#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179438 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/id #uuid "622bd0ff-d08a-5504-8b38-8bc388bfcbf5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12525" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x=x_1$ to $x=x_2$ is given by\n\n$$\nW = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} F(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ at a rate of $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ is\n\n$$\nF = ma = 3 \\cdot (1 + 2x^2) = (3 + 6x^2) \\,\\rm{N}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3+6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[3x+2{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(2)+2({2}^{3})-(3(0)+2({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =6+16-0 \\\\  & =22\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179439 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "4e428ee4-0017-5e83-887c-26e723b4af08" :question/math-academy-id "e-12526" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(4t+1)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t = 1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t = 2\\,\\rm{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity $v_0,$ and final velocity $v_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =2{t}^{2}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0) = 0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0 = 2(0^2) + 0 + C\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nC = 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = 2t^2 + t.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t = 1\\,\\rm{s}$ and final velocity at time $t = 2\\,\\rm{s}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=2({1}^{2})+1=3\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=2({2}^{2})+2=10\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =100-9 \\\\  & =91\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179440 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/id #uuid "39eab788-1a8d-51af-b535-594cacc8ec2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12527" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $(t^2-2t+3)\\,\\rm{m},$ where $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t=3\\,\\rm{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW\n= \n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)=t^2-2t+3,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+3) \\\\  & =2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\nv_0 = v(1) =  2(1) -2 = 0\\,\\rm{m/s},\n$$\n\nand its final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\nv_1 = v(3) = 2(3) -2= 4\\,\\rm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{2} \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179441 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "8f79d7e9-377f-5012-a762-279839fc806a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12528" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(x+3)\\,\\rm{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW\n= \n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=2$ to $x=4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+3x]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =(8+12)-(2+6) \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity $v_1$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 12 & ={v}_{1}^{2}-4 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $4\\,\\rm{m/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b968cb6-6ae9-5b0c-9af4-6dd1a21d39c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416155} {:answer/id #uuid "469e84d9-10e8-5bb8-8014-4bea0369b746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416160} {:answer/id #uuid "6afd05e0-6e50-556e-93ad-2b191ada987b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416176} {:answer/id #uuid "8b758d59-3144-5ba6-9ee7-ab705a9dc58e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416189} {:answer/id #uuid "ebdc4bfd-1d77-5007-a2a4-775aba359b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b968cb6-6ae9-5b0c-9af4-6dd1a21d39c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416155} :answer-field/id #uuid "188254e6-7bf6-52e7-85b7-577eb0a8ea66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "856810c6-e294-5790-8bc1-67fd3e665a84" :question/math-academy-id "q-163597" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $1.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=1.5\\cdot (2x+1)=(3x+1.5)\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(3x+1.5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({2}^{2}+2-({1}^{2}+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(6-2) \\\\  & =6\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c59d75c-f137-5ad0-adb1-d98444184b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416144} {:answer/id #uuid "112a9bb0-329a-50bc-862b-f1f1908e9ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416146} {:answer/id #uuid "832666e4-bfb7-5311-860f-b9c7edf6df68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416186} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a7e4a3-4075-524e-b699-5f80dc6c213a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416210} {:answer/id #uuid "ef25fa57-c8c1-5068-8f4b-f19cebf60716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "832666e4-bfb7-5311-860f-b9c7edf6df68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416186} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb6dfeb2-3a92-53e8-a0f6-ee617bbb1e9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "babebacc-c620-5336-84f2-4d7aa7dcc6d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-163598" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $2t\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & ={t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0={0}^{2}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2)={2}^{2}=4\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9085cdd3-9d32-5301-8cbf-ca557b3f00d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416192} {:answer/id #uuid "93b0a24f-9f97-5f8e-ae03-d64c89bef3e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416194} {:answer/id #uuid "c4de12ba-3316-5e1b-ac4d-ff0026c7af55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416214} {:answer/id #uuid "c8dba406-896d-56df-ba18-d38eb5a20778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416217} {:answer/id #uuid "fab72cab-a457-504c-bec5-6f9514a2cbb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4de12ba-3316-5e1b-ac4d-ff0026c7af55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416214} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a75307-ee16-5df7-9ca9-2990c5ceb006" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93920b26-fb04-52d5-8d58-1e17f6af55c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-163663" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $({t}^{2}+t+1)\\,m,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=0\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)={t}^{2}+t+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t+1) \\\\  & =2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=0$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(0)=2(0)+1=1\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=2(2)+1=5\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {5}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{75}{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =36\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a230f6-8610-57b2-a192-bbc685af3d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416147} {:answer/id #uuid "2771a33d-a580-5640-b992-3faea8d28c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416151} {:answer/id #uuid "45f0d404-cb57-5e93-863a-e6e0439ae329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416158} {:answer/id #uuid "b365bf0a-2cb2-5bc5-a6c1-23cd8ffbd67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416205} {:answer/id #uuid "f5298386-5d77-55b8-8b1f-b593564733bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b365bf0a-2cb2-5bc5-a6c1-23cd8ffbd67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416205} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee421f31-693d-5e9b-9b13-f8f8930e49f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f5f6801-1e10-5d42-880a-19ee72d417ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-163665" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $6x\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}6x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3(4-1) \\\\  & =9\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=2$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\ 9 & ={v}_{1}^{2}-16 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=2$ is $5\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31c287bc-7b24-588a-a540-58a1e566a1b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416153} {:answer/id #uuid "6f79dc59-3bf2-54c9-9f6d-145c24052071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416179} {:answer/id #uuid "76dbeeb4-610b-5130-aaee-b66603d18018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416181} {:answer/id #uuid "98f49da7-f783-5dd3-b704-89fad4575d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416196} {:answer/id #uuid "cc674083-0796-5e90-b9c6-07a3221072a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f79dc59-3bf2-54c9-9f6d-145c24052071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416179} :answer-field/id #uuid "29c62bd1-d614-5073-873e-5b53441e5434" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "841964b0-d48f-583c-9b1a-d5d38816deaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-163666" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2})\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters, the particle is initially at the origin, and the particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=2,$ find the particle's change in velocity in moving from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =16+16-0 \\\\  & =32\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=5\\,m/s$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=0$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 32 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {5}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 32 & =50-2{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2{v}_{0}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=0$ is $3\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=5-3=2\\,m/s.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5af78eb7-b0ac-5fb4-8298-83843c06cabd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416170} {:answer/id #uuid "7a400848-d283-509d-b2ce-69f7f8a4002f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416183} {:answer/id #uuid "86e20970-7c3b-5405-aba7-818b64498280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416187} {:answer/id #uuid "c6037124-9ca8-59a2-a5ba-448ad5035b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416215} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e877fb-7b90-52aa-a3a8-3a656bf7b8f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86e20970-7c3b-5405-aba7-818b64498280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416187} :answer-field/id #uuid "685ebe43-4362-5e9f-b513-b366e7e5ddc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c1be8fb-89db-5bdf-8f42-bf7da0962b48" :question/math-academy-id "q-163667" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(3{x}^{2}+1)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at the origin, and the particle has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+1-0 \\\\  & =2\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=0$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {4}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =8-\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=0$ is $2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70f35aeb-2053-5b37-abce-48d3972b397c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389437} {:answer/id #uuid "24f06dbe-e866-519e-8599-01c0ead2742e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389438} {:answer/id #uuid "7a155385-200c-550f-96bf-08d5a75d20fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389439} {:answer/id #uuid "318dfa74-61f2-571e-8498-0ef0bc26ce59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389440} {:answer/id #uuid "156b8685-b780-52be-a010-0b4b0806771e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "156b8685-b780-52be-a010-0b4b0806771e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389441} :answer-field/id #uuid "c13537c9-cba9-528c-81df-ec823b958306" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8305d2ef-8fb9-5f80-b2fe-3b5b2380f4bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-163671" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $(t+1)\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=2\\,s$ and $t=4\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=t+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(2)=2+1=3\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=4+1=5\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (5)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (3)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =8\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "583dd30f-780b-569e-92c3-cc4ee471b53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416169} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec9126b-bae7-5d30-87eb-6515e3d60873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "118\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416198} {:answer/id #uuid "b711d539-6b80-51e9-bad0-dc087d68179d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416209} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3cb8b5-896e-5b5e-a89a-10510757b28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416221} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a04a41-69d2-572c-85f9-3f8ecf5abeb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4a04a41-69d2-572c-85f9-3f8ecf5abeb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416224} :answer-field/id #uuid "440ab6c6-89d8-5d73-9c6d-94b052c4135f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6813bd80-c67c-56a5-8d0d-2e4e5ee1b618" :question/math-academy-id "q-163672" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $(3t-1)\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=3\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=3t-1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=3(1)-1=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(3)=3(3)-1=8\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (8)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =96-6 \\\\  & =90\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "245f5f16-c1db-52b8-93a7-c27b81880f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "625\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416150} {:answer/id #uuid "357620ce-cca0-59e5-a668-2ee27ee5034a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "735\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416154} {:answer/id #uuid "3f12cf32-2fc6-5ed9-9aec-bed26735d3c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "660\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416156} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb1f837-d5d5-5a17-b24a-c680b3267dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "585\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416190} {:answer/id #uuid "cd33b190-ed48-5fec-9293-b65a62708603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "530\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cb1f837-d5d5-5a17-b24a-c680b3267dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "585\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416190} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bd8f27e-7cc0-5e2f-951e-b5461e6ef25c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34ad571d-a040-5724-a4dd-5d1ff6b4a0c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163674" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $({t}^{3}+2)\\,m,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=2\\,s$ and $t=3\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)={t}^{3}+2,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+2) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(2)=3(2)^{2}=12\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(3)=3(3)^{2}=27\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (27)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (12)^{2} \\\\  & =729-144 \\\\  & =585\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d7dfd5d-6a26-57a3-9520-0e5f3f2556d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416177} {:answer/id #uuid "9f10d917-ef03-5eb9-9451-45b13e37761c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416200} {:answer/id #uuid "d47e9cdf-700c-5733-a11d-78b0ff44a038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416223} {:answer/id #uuid "dda5a39a-b3d7-53c3-a43b-5f036f3bb6db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416227} {:answer/id #uuid "efd1ddb6-ca2b-5d57-8cbb-36a4723bcb03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "108\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d47e9cdf-700c-5733-a11d-78b0ff44a038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416223} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a61099-7c6c-5889-a8f0-36c27d613946" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d148a18c-482f-58b6-ae26-c16e793a32c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163676" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $0.25\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $({t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+2)\\,m,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)={t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+2,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=4(1)^{3}-4(1)=0\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=4(2)^{3}-4(2)=24\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0.25\\cdot (24)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0.25\\cdot {0}^{2} \\\\  & =72-0 \\\\  & =72\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f15786c-7b03-54e6-8703-5ed8327eeb6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416165} {:answer/id #uuid "917167c2-f28e-5235-8996-88a317199397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416193} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2d71f8-f520-5950-a0fc-eaf5e6853af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416211} {:answer/id #uuid "e9148945-fdec-553f-9907-9d2484cbc0ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416229} {:answer/id #uuid "f75d04ef-9f29-5e3a-9b0c-022da6954dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "917167c2-f28e-5235-8996-88a317199397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416193} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0260c4e-12d5-5629-b2d4-7c1539134366" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc2329ee-2286-5cc1-b9c4-aaae4fcfd160" :question/math-academy-id "q-163682" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(2-x)\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(2-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2(2)-\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{2})-(2(1)-\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{2})) \\\\  & =4-2-2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e01e9e2-099f-5829-bb90-988331fa2c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416178} {:answer/id #uuid "944d02a9-bd37-50e5-8f3a-13e10492e0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416195} {:answer/id #uuid "afa584ba-d9c9-5af5-875d-b8761e92e385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416203} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2651a2-6940-5df5-b549-821bd938e2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416213} {:answer/id #uuid "d88f3206-b7d8-5454-a54b-c8dbf5728dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf2651a2-6940-5df5-b549-821bd938e2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416213} :answer-field/id #uuid "e533a5eb-946c-53fd-b9db-7dca4ec6140a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49e23a3c-f5ee-5f32-8207-650dc0b4ca07" :question/math-academy-id "q-163686" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(3\\sqrt{x}+2)\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3\\sqrt{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{1/2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3/2}+2x]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2({4}^{3/2})+2(4)-(2({1}^{3/2})+2(1)) \\\\  & =16+8-2-2 \\\\  & =20\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a35d382-4f29-517d-8811-38dd718f3949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416152} {:answer/id #uuid "6a609468-13c4-5167-adfa-d9641c742919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416174} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb1a335-16df-592b-9aa2-30cdc25d7546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416185} {:answer/id #uuid "9c755c70-2778-53b1-aa2d-fd52ee0d754b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416197} {:answer/id #uuid "d8495461-fbf1-5db5-90d6-ecc9dfa46e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a609468-13c4-5167-adfa-d9641c742919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416174} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecf8557a-f127-5151-95f4-647459864ef1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0be1c169-3c4d-5e81-bcd5-f050f3ef61f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163687" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(8-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(8-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[8x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =8(2)-\\frac{1}{4}({2}^{4})-(8(1)-\\frac{1}{4}({1}^{4})) \\\\  & =16-4-8+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179777 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ed38dcf-d565-5c8f-a354-7f15129024b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416148} {:answer/id #uuid "4906765c-cf5e-56de-b7cc-bbfeff0afa63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416162} {:answer/id #uuid "78e2fabc-a95e-59e5-9e2c-304ec9d164ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416182} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2f1ede-2cc2-54bb-8896-41a7f39dd5ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416184} {:answer/id #uuid "fc464ecd-6249-5343-9b2d-d1ebf3f3d163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e2f1ede-2cc2-54bb-8896-41a7f39dd5ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416184} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d665a14-f9c6-5b0e-9f42-6dea1fa5cd93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "539e0059-24fe-5c3a-b9b6-abd2934de330" :question/math-academy-id "q-163688" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $0.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(5-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $0.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $(5-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=0.5\\cdot (5-x)=(2.5-0.5x)\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(2.5-0.5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(2)-\\frac{1}{4}({2}^{2})-(\\frac{5}{2}(1)-\\frac{1}{4}({1}^{2})) \\\\  & =5-1-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23a970a6-fde1-5b7d-91a5-9f491fb2d37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416149} {:answer/id #uuid "4862b45c-aff1-5dd0-bdea-a37244d8264e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416161} {:answer/id #uuid "4f969a37-7f40-5d87-a34d-979e387d5920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416166} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ddfe8f-5177-5ba3-bcf9-6389f879b188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416206} {:answer/id #uuid "b63eb1f1-da1c-50d8-9fa0-a62c1a301b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4ddfe8f-5177-5ba3-bcf9-6389f879b188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416206} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c056e2-1426-5bcb-a501-ebc6f3a55d75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e08604c1-4714-50ad-b1df-f5621c3586f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-163691" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $3{x}^{2}\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ at $x=0,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{3}-0 \\\\  & =8\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ at $x=0.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=2$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =2{v}_{1}^{2}-8 \\\\ 2{v}_{1}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=2$ is $2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4688ab13-3768-5564-a3fb-bdcb6748fe3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416159} {:answer/id #uuid "542437df-028e-59d6-ae86-f7227217dfb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416167} {:answer/id #uuid "673d5641-d0ad-5ff6-a052-0f9b30a96ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416172} {:answer/id #uuid "70095663-b0c7-5784-aa18-5da9b912ed20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416180} {:answer/id #uuid "a42ed274-a649-5401-8272-60c7780ed481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a42ed274-a649-5401-8272-60c7780ed481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416201} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9005e61-607d-5045-aa66-20814a697f93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a80bea3f-8cb9-59ff-8c63-3325a8f0a338" :question/math-academy-id "q-163703" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters, the particle is initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=4,$ find the particle's change in velocity in moving from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[3x+\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(12+\\frac{1}{4})-(3+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{4}\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=4.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{33}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {7}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{33}{4} & =\\frac{49}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{v}_{0}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $4\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=1$ to $x=4$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=7-4=3\\,m/s.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44790b19-9ced-5d46-bcb9-825594809176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416157} {:answer/id #uuid "6215fee1-a677-5186-a3d4-62cb7223b14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416171} {:answer/id #uuid "b234e6b8-43be-5423-be11-ece81f246d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416204} {:answer/id #uuid "b60dbd39-c84e-5d27-aee9-c2f06ea806f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416207} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b9a351-f8a5-58dc-901e-b298299b5029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b234e6b8-43be-5423-be11-ece81f246d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416204} :answer-field/id #uuid "915e3c86-f8a0-5f78-befd-32698839d7d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdc532fe-5376-523b-b4c1-4852cb3ef3ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-163704" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $8\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=3\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =-\\frac{1}{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=-\\frac{1}{0+1}+C\\,⟹\\,C=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=1-\\frac{1}{t+1}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=3\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(3)=1-\\frac{1}{3+1}=\\frac{3}{4}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016d8147-2b48-53a6-82af-fafe816ab49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416143} {:answer/id #uuid "4e46b4a9-a4e3-5f5f-80ed-268038e11e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416163} {:answer/id #uuid "8f693876-d062-56a4-8e01-a7786aea86f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416191} {:answer/id #uuid "9eea50ba-01cb-55aa-9a12-e59c66a5bac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416199} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d6d1ac-3382-5896-9f56-457c7886e3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "016d8147-2b48-53a6-82af-fafe816ab49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416143} :answer-field/id #uuid "33c85393-49c2-59b3-86c8-fb54c6c913c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a6ac8ce-563c-538e-a4b6-829308c43be7" :question/math-academy-id "q-163706" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $3\\sqrt{t}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=1\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 3\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =2{t}^{3/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=2({0}^{3/2})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{3/2}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(1)=2({1}^{3/2})=2\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =6\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ec6c244-5362-5ac9-9cb2-e52010f2e97e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416164} {:answer/id #uuid "562735b2-f2b9-5808-9e01-b4da0083cca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416168} {:answer/id #uuid "6a368a44-5bac-522d-9eaa-44cf19404a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416173} {:answer/id #uuid "c924bbae-ceed-5733-99b9-207c3ea0be7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416218} {:answer/id #uuid "e23afd90-794c-56ba-9d78-eb6940eb6376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e23afd90-794c-56ba-9d78-eb6940eb6376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416228} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5e2fc07-70cb-5248-ae29-9ab2d781882c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "115c519a-042d-5002-84f6-473dd06e56ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-163709" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $\\frac{3}{2}\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(1+2t)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (1+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =t+{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=0+{0}^{2}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=t+{t}^{2}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2)=2+{2}^{2}=6\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 36 \\\\  & =27\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd6aeb4-c566-5a88-9e32-388d6c8dfea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416145} {:answer/id #uuid "6ace19a1-23ee-5f18-97e5-036b1b6c5ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416175} {:answer/id #uuid "87c5b30a-40f0-5260-b4b3-8c31092ef6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416188} {:answer/id #uuid "a71705a5-1eed-57a7-9c98-5053c71a7ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416202} {:answer/id #uuid "bca90054-3073-5e42-9916-d9655036d407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bca90054-3073-5e42-9916-d9655036d407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416212} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1a23531-d7b7-5e92-b4ab-f45652f32a5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2dec6491-4f2e-5985-9f0d-5f140c0cfaf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163713" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $\\frac{1}{t+1}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=3\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{1}{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0+1|+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t+1|.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=3\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+1|=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(3)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3+1|=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2} \\\\  & =8{ln}^{2}⁡2-2{ln}^{2}⁡2 \\\\  & =6{ln}^{2}⁡(2)\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
